# expect: parse ok
# description: large real-world e-commerce platform covering all 10 node types, relationships, orchestration, memory, code, verify, agent_context, load_profiles, temporal fields
agm: 1.0
package: ecommerce.platform
version: 2.3.1
title: E-Commerce Platform Core Architecture
owner: platform-engineering
description:
  Comprehensive architecture for a multi-tenant e-commerce platform
  handling product catalog, order lifecycle, payment processing,
  inventory management, and customer experience. Designed for
  horizontal scalability with event-driven microservices.
status: active
default_load: operational
tags: [ecommerce, microservices, event-driven, multi-tenant]
target_runtime: octopus
load_profiles:
  minimal:
    filter: "priority in [critical] AND type in [decision, facts]"
    estimated_tokens: 1200
  operational:
    filter: "priority in [critical, high] AND type in [workflow, decision, facts, rules, entity]"
    estimated_tokens: 6500
  executable:
    filter: "type in [workflow, orchestration] AND code is present"
    estimated_tokens: 8200
  full:
    filter: "*"
    estimated_tokens: 14000

node platform.stack
type: facts
stability: high
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [stack, infrastructure]
summary: rust backend services; next15 storefront; postgresql primary store; redis cache; kafka event bus; s3 media storage
items:
  - api_gateway=rust_axum
  - catalog_service=rust_axum
  - order_service=rust_axum
  - payment_service=rust_axum
  - inventory_service=rust_axum
  - storefront=next15_app_router
  - database=postgresql_16
  - cache=redis_7
  - event_bus=kafka_3
  - media_storage=s3_compatible
  - search=elasticsearch_8
  - observability=opentelemetry_grafana
detail:
  Backend services use Rust with Axum framework for high throughput
  and memory safety. The storefront is built on Next.js 15 with App
  Router for SSR and ISR capabilities. PostgreSQL is the primary
  data store with read replicas per region. Redis handles session
  caching, rate limiting, and ephemeral data. Kafka provides the
  event backbone for eventual consistency between bounded contexts.
  S3-compatible storage is used for product images, invoices, and
  media assets. Elasticsearch powers the product search with
  faceted filtering and relevance scoring.
notes:
  Infrastructure runs on Kubernetes with Helm charts managed in a
  separate infra repository. Each service has its own database schema
  isolated by PostgreSQL schemas within a shared cluster.

node platform.constraints
type: rules
stability: high
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [security, compliance, architecture]
summary: idempotent mutations; event sourcing for orders; pci-dss for payments; gdpr data handling; rate limiting on all public endpoints
items:
  - all_write_operations_must_be_idempotent
  - order_state_transitions_must_be_event_sourced
  - payment_data_must_comply_with_pci_dss
  - personal_data_handling_must_comply_with_gdpr
  - rate_limiting_required_on_all_public_endpoints
  - cross_service_calls_must_use_circuit_breakers
  - database_migrations_must_be_backward_compatible
  - api_versions_must_follow_semver
  - all_services_must_emit_structured_logs
  - telemetry_traces_must_propagate_across_service_boundaries
detail:
  These constraints are non-negotiable and apply to all services in the
  platform. Violations are caught by CI lint checks and architecture
  fitness functions. PCI-DSS compliance requires that raw card numbers
  never touch our systems; all payment processing goes through the
  payment gateway tokenization flow. GDPR requires right-to-erasure
  support within 30 days of request.

node catalog.product
type: entity
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [catalog, domain-model]
depends: [platform.stack]
summary: product aggregate root with variants, pricing tiers, media gallery, and category associations
fields:
  - product_id: uuid primary key
  - sku: string unique per tenant
  - name: string max 200 chars
  - slug: string url-safe unique per tenant
  - description: text markdown-enabled
  - brand: string optional
  - categories: array of category references
  - variants: array of product_variant embedded
  - pricing: pricing_context embedded
  - media: array of media_asset references
  - attributes: json key-value map
  - status: enum (draft, active, archived, discontinued)
  - tenant_id: uuid partition key
  - created_at: timestamp with timezone
  - updated_at: timestamp with timezone
  - published_at: timestamp with timezone nullable
  - seo_title: string max 70 chars nullable
  - seo_description: string max 160 chars nullable
detail:
  Products are the central aggregate in the catalog bounded context.
  Each product belongs to exactly one tenant and can have multiple
  variants (size, color, material). Pricing is context-dependent:
  base price, member price, bulk pricing tiers. The media gallery
  supports images, videos, and 360-degree views with CDN delivery.
  Elasticsearch indexing is triggered asynchronously via Kafka events
  on product mutations.

node catalog.search.rules
type: rules
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [catalog, search]
depends: [catalog.product]
summary: search relevance rules for product discovery including facets, synonyms, and boosting
items:
  - exact_sku_match_boosts_to_top
  - name_match_weighted_3x_over_description
  - in_stock_products_boosted_over_out_of_stock
  - category_facets_always_available
  - price_range_facets_use_dynamic_intervals
  - brand_facets_sorted_by_frequency
  - synonym_expansion_applied_before_scoring
  - minimum_should_match_75_percent_for_multi_term
  - typo_tolerance_enabled_for_queries_over_4_chars
  - recently_published_products_get_freshness_boost
detail:
  Search tuning is critical for conversion. The relevance model uses
  Elasticsearch function_score queries combining text relevance, stock
  status, recency, and sales velocity signals.

node order.lifecycle
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [orders, lifecycle, event-sourcing]
depends: [platform.constraints, catalog.product]
related_to: [payment.process, inventory.reserve]
input: [cart_items, customer_id, shipping_address, payment_method_token]
output: [order_id, order_confirmation_event]
summary: create order -> validate stock -> reserve inventory -> process payment -> confirm order -> emit events
steps:
  - validate cart items exist and are purchasable
  - calculate order totals with tax and shipping
  - apply discount codes and loyalty points
  - reserve inventory for all line items
  - initiate payment authorization
  - on payment success create order record
  - emit order.confirmed event to kafka
  - send confirmation email via notification service
  - schedule fulfillment task
  - on payment failure release inventory reservations
detail:
  The order lifecycle is event-sourced: every state transition produces
  an immutable event appended to the order event stream. The current
  state is reconstructed by replaying events. This enables full audit
  trails, temporal queries, and compensation for failed operations.
  The workflow uses the saga pattern for distributed transaction
  coordination across inventory, payment, and order services.
notes:
  Cart validation re-checks prices at order creation time to prevent
  stale-price exploits. The payment authorization is held for 7 days
  before auto-void if fulfillment doesn't begin.

node order.states
type: glossary
stability: high
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [orders, glossary]
depends: [order.lifecycle]
summary: canonical order status definitions for the order lifecycle state machine
items:
  - pending: order created but payment not yet attempted
  - payment_authorized: payment hold placed, awaiting fulfillment
  - processing: order accepted and being prepared for shipment
  - shipped: carrier has the package, tracking number assigned
  - delivered: carrier confirmed delivery at address
  - cancelled: order cancelled before shipment
  - refunded: payment returned to customer after cancellation or return
  - return_requested: customer initiated a return
  - return_received: returned items received at warehouse
  - partially_fulfilled: some items shipped, others pending
aliases:
  - order statuses
  - order state machine

node payment.process
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [payments, pci, integration]
depends: [platform.constraints]
related_to: [order.lifecycle]
input: [payment_method_token, amount, currency, order_id, idempotency_key]
output: [transaction_id, authorization_code, payment_status]
summary: tokenized payment flow through gateway with idempotency, retry logic, and webhook reconciliation
steps:
  - validate payment_method_token format and expiry
  - lookup idempotency_key to detect duplicate requests
  - send authorization request to payment gateway
  - handle gateway response codes
  - persist transaction record with gateway reference
  - emit payment.authorized or payment.failed event
  - register webhook handler for async settlement notifications
  - on settlement webhook update transaction status
  - reconcile daily batch with gateway settlement reports
detail:
  Payment processing never handles raw card data. The storefront
  collects card details via the gateway's PCI-compliant JavaScript
  SDK which returns a one-time-use token. Our backend sends this
  token to the gateway API for authorization. Idempotency keys
  prevent duplicate charges on network retries.
scope: [production, staging]

node payment.gateway.timeout
type: exception
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [payments, resilience]
depends: [payment.process]
summary: payment gateway timeout or 5xx during authorization requires idempotent retry with exponential backoff
detail:
  Gateway timeouts are common during peak traffic. The payment was
  potentially processed but we didn't receive confirmation. Blind
  retry without idempotency would cause double charges.
resolution:
  - retry with same idempotency_key using exponential backoff
  - maximum 3 retries over 30 seconds
  - if all retries fail mark payment as requires_manual_review
  - alert on-call if failure rate exceeds 5 percent in 5 minutes
  - never assume payment failed without gateway confirmation
  - use gateway query API to check authorization status before final determination

node payment.browser.storage
type: anti_pattern
stability: high
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [security, anti-pattern, payments]
conflicts: [platform.constraints]
summary: storing payment tokens or card data in browser storage violates pci-dss and exposes customer financial data
detail:
  localStorage, sessionStorage, or cookies must never contain payment
  tokens, card numbers, CVVs, or any PCI-sensitive data. Even
  tokenized payment method references should have short TTLs and be
  transmitted only over HTTPS. XSS vulnerabilities would directly
  expose financial data if stored client-side.

node inventory.reserve
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [inventory, warehouse]
depends: [platform.stack]
related_to: [order.lifecycle]
input: [line_items, warehouse_id]
output: [reservation_id, reserved_quantities]
summary: atomic inventory reservation with optimistic locking and automatic expiration
steps:
  - select optimal warehouse by proximity and stock levels
  - acquire optimistic lock on inventory records
  - verify sufficient available quantity for each sku
  - decrement available quantity and increment reserved quantity
  - create reservation record with 15 minute expiry
  - emit inventory.reserved event
  - schedule reservation expiry check
code:
  lang: sql
  target: services/inventory/migrations/004_reservation_table.sql
  action: create
  body: |
    CREATE TABLE inventory_reservations (
        reservation_id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
        order_id UUID NOT NULL,
        sku VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
        warehouse_id UUID NOT NULL,
        quantity INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (quantity > 0),
        status VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'
            CHECK (status IN ('active', 'released', 'committed', 'expired')),
        expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW() + INTERVAL '15 minutes',
        created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),
        CONSTRAINT fk_warehouse FOREIGN KEY (warehouse_id)
            REFERENCES warehouses(warehouse_id)
    );

    CREATE INDEX idx_reservations_expiry
        ON inventory_reservations (expires_at)
        WHERE status = 'active';

    CREATE INDEX idx_reservations_order
        ON inventory_reservations (order_id);
verify:
  - type: file_exists
    file: services/inventory/migrations/004_reservation_table.sql
  - type: file_contains
    file: services/inventory/migrations/004_reservation_table.sql
    pattern: "CREATE TABLE inventory_reservations"
  - type: command
    run: cargo check -p inventory-service
    expect: exit_code_0
agent_context:
  load_nodes: [platform.stack, platform.constraints]
  load_files:
    - path: services/inventory/src/models/reservation.rs
      range: full
    - path: services/inventory/src/handlers/reserve.rs
      range: [1, 80]
  system_hint: "PostgreSQL 16. Use advisory locks for hot SKUs. Reservation TTL is configurable via env."
  max_tokens: 5000
execution_status: pending

node customer.session
type: entity
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [customer, session, security]
depends: [platform.stack]
summary: customer session with opaque token, cart reference, tenant isolation, and device fingerprint
fields:
  - session_id: uuid primary key
  - customer_id: uuid nullable for anonymous sessions
  - tenant_id: uuid partition key
  - cart_id: uuid nullable
  - device_fingerprint: string hashed
  - ip_address: inet
  - user_agent: string truncated to 500 chars
  - auth_level: enum (anonymous, authenticated, elevated)
  - created_at: timestamp with timezone
  - last_active_at: timestamp with timezone
  - expires_at: timestamp with timezone
scope: [production, staging]
valid_from: 2024-01-15
valid_until: 2026-12-31

node customer.loyalty
type: facts
stability: low
priority: normal
confidence: medium
tags: [customer, loyalty, marketing]
depends: [customer.session]
summary: loyalty program tiers with point accumulation rates, redemption rules, and tier qualification thresholds
items:
  - tier_bronze=0_to_999_points
  - tier_silver=1000_to_4999_points
  - tier_gold=5000_to_19999_points
  - tier_platinum=20000_plus_points
  - earn_rate_base=1_point_per_dollar
  - earn_rate_silver=1.5x_multiplier
  - earn_rate_gold=2x_multiplier
  - earn_rate_platinum=3x_multiplier
  - redemption_ratio=100_points_equals_1_dollar
  - points_expire_after_18_months_inactivity
  - tier_evaluation_quarterly
detail:
  The loyalty program is a key retention mechanism. Points accumulate
  on every purchase and can be redeemed as discount at checkout. Tier
  benefits include free shipping thresholds, early access to sales,
  and dedicated support channels.

node checkout.pricing.decision
type: decision
stability: high
priority: critical
confidence: high
tags: [pricing, architecture]
depends: [catalog.product, customer.loyalty, platform.constraints]
conflicts: [payment.browser.storage]
summary: server-side pricing calculation with real-time discount engine instead of client-side price resolution
rationale:
  - prevents price manipulation through client-side tampering
  - enables complex discount stacking rules server-side
  - supports flash sales with sub-second price updates
  - audit trail for every price computation
  - consistent pricing across storefront and api channels
tradeoffs:
  - adds latency to cart operations
  - pricing service becomes critical path dependency
  - requires caching strategy for high-traffic product pages
  - complicates offline mode for pos terminals

node checkout.discount.example
type: example
stability: medium
priority: normal
confidence: high
tags: [pricing, examples]
depends: [checkout.pricing.decision]
summary: example discount stacking calculation showing coupon, loyalty, and volume discount interaction
detail:
  Gold tier customer with 5000 points purchases 3 units of Product X
  (base price $100 each) with coupon SAVE20. Base total is 3 x $100
  = $300.00. Volume discount (3+ units) applies -5% = -$15.00 giving
  $285.00. Coupon SAVE20 applies -20% on remaining = -$57.00 giving
  $228.00. Loyalty points (500 redeemed) deducts $5.00 giving $223.00.
  Tax (8.5%) adds $18.96 giving $241.96. Free shipping applies for
  Gold tier with order above $150. Final amount is $241.96 with 500
  loyalty points deducted and 446 new points earned (2x Gold multiplier
  on $223.00 pre-tax amount).

node shipping.estimation
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: medium
tags: [shipping, logistics]
depends: [inventory.reserve, customer.session]
input: [shipping_address, line_items, warehouse_locations]
output: [shipping_options, estimated_delivery_dates, shipping_costs]
summary: calculate shipping options by comparing carrier rates, warehouse proximity, and delivery speed tiers
steps:
  - determine fulfillment warehouse for each line item
  - calculate package dimensions and weight
  - query carrier rate apis in parallel for each warehouse-destination pair
  - apply shipping promotions and free shipping thresholds
  - estimate delivery windows based on carrier sla and cutoff times
  - present sorted options by speed and cost
  - cache rates for 15 minutes keyed by destination zip and package profile
memory:
  - action: upsert
    topic: shipping.rates
    key: shipping.rate.cache.last.updated
    value: timestamp of last carrier rate refresh
    scope: session
    ttl: 900
  - action: get
    topic: shipping.carriers
    key: shipping.carrier.preferences
    scope: project

node notification.templates
type: facts
stability: low
priority: normal
confidence: medium
tags: [notifications, email, sms]
related_to: [order.lifecycle]
summary: transactional notification templates for order events including email, sms, and push channels
items:
  - order_confirmation_email
  - payment_receipt_email
  - shipment_tracking_email
  - delivery_confirmation_email
  - return_initiated_email
  - refund_processed_email
  - cart_abandonment_email
  - welcome_series_email
  - password_reset_email
  - order_shipped_sms
  - delivery_eta_sms
  - flash_sale_push
  - back_in_stock_push

node analytics.tracking.rules
type: rules
stability: medium
priority: normal
confidence: medium
tags: [analytics, tracking, privacy]
depends: [platform.constraints]
summary: analytics event tracking rules with consent management, data minimization, and retention policies
items:
  - all_tracking_requires_explicit_consent
  - consent_state_checked_on_every_page_load
  - pii_stripped_before_analytics_ingestion
  - session_events_aggregated_before_warehouse_load
  - raw_events_retained_90_days
  - aggregated_data_retained_3_years
  - ab_test_assignments_stored_with_events
  - funnel_events_must_include_step_index
  - revenue_events_must_include_currency
  - cross_device_tracking_requires_authenticated_identity
applies_when: storefront sessions where analytics consent is granted

node api.versioning.decision
type: decision
stability: high
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [api, versioning, architecture]
depends: [platform.constraints]
see_also: [platform.stack]
summary: url-path versioning with sunset headers instead of content-negotiation versioning
rationale:
  - url-path versioning is simpler for api consumers
  - easier to route at load balancer level
  - clearer documentation with version in url
  - sunset header provides deprecation timeline
tradeoffs:
  - url space grows with each version
  - harder to support partial version upgrades
  - clients must update base urls on major version change
keywords: [api-design, versioning-strategy, deprecation]

node platform.glossary
type: glossary
stability: high
priority: normal
confidence: high
tags: [glossary, domain-language]
summary: ubiquitous language definitions for the e-commerce platform domain
items:
  - sku: stock keeping unit, unique product variant identifier within a tenant
  - tenant: isolated business entity sharing the platform infrastructure
  - cart: temporary collection of items a customer intends to purchase
  - order: committed purchase with payment and fulfillment tracking
  - reservation: temporary hold on inventory pending payment confirmation
  - authorization: payment hold without capture, funds earmarked but not transferred
  - capture: actual transfer of authorized payment funds
  - settlement: daily reconciliation of captures with payment gateway
  - fulfillment: process of picking, packing, and shipping an order
  - sku velocity: rate of sales per sku per time period used for demand forecasting
  - dead stock: inventory with zero sales velocity over 90 days
  - backorder: order accepted for out-of-stock items with delayed fulfillment
aliases:
  - ecommerce dictionary
  - domain glossary

node deploy.canary.workflow
type: workflow
stability: medium
priority: high
confidence: high
tags: [deployment, canary, observability]
depends: [platform.stack, platform.constraints]
input: [service_name, new_version, canary_percentage]
output: [deployment_status, rollback_decision]
summary: canary deployment with traffic splitting, metric comparison, and automatic rollback on error rate threshold
steps:
  - build and push container image for new version
  - deploy canary pod with 5 percent traffic weight
  - monitor error rate and p99 latency for 10 minutes
  - if error rate above 1 percent or p99 above 500ms trigger rollback
  - if metrics healthy increase traffic to 25 percent
  - monitor for additional 10 minutes
  - if still healthy promote to 100 percent
  - drain old version pods
  - emit deployment.completed event
code_blocks:
  - lang: yaml
    target: deploy/helm/templates/canary.yaml
    action: create
    body: |
      apiVersion: flagger.app/v1beta1
      kind: Canary
      metadata:
        name: {{ .Values.service.name }}
        namespace: {{ .Release.Namespace }}
      spec:
        targetRef:
          apiVersion: apps/v1
          kind: Deployment
          name: {{ .Values.service.name }}
        progressDeadlineSeconds: 600
        analysis:
          interval: 60s
          threshold: 5
          maxWeight: 50
          stepWeight: 10
          metrics:
            - name: request-success-rate
              thresholdRange:
                min: 99
              interval: 60s
            - name: request-duration
              thresholdRange:
                max: 500
              interval: 60s
  - lang: bash
    target: scripts/deploy-canary.sh
    action: create
    body: |
      #!/usr/bin/env bash
      set -euo pipefail

      SERVICE_NAME="${1:?Usage: deploy-canary.sh <service> <version>}"
      VERSION="${2:?Usage: deploy-canary.sh <service> <version>}"

      echo "Deploying $SERVICE_NAME v$VERSION via canary..."
      helm upgrade --install "$SERVICE_NAME" deploy/helm \
        --set image.tag="$VERSION" \
        --set canary.enabled=true \
        --namespace production \
        --wait --timeout 300s

      echo "Canary deployed. Monitoring via Flagger..."
verify:
  - type: file_exists
    file: deploy/helm/templates/canary.yaml
  - type: file_exists
    file: scripts/deploy-canary.sh
  - type: command
    run: helm lint deploy/helm
    expect: exit_code_0
  - type: file_contains
    file: deploy/helm/templates/canary.yaml
    pattern: "kind: Canary"
agent_context:
  load_nodes: [platform.stack, platform.constraints]
  load_files:
    - path: deploy/helm/values.yaml
      range: full
    - path: deploy/helm/Chart.yaml
      range: full
  system_hint: "Kubernetes 1.28. Flagger for progressive delivery. Helm 3.14+."
  max_tokens: 6000
execution_status: pending

node platform.monitoring.example
type: example
stability: medium
priority: normal
confidence: medium
tags: [observability, examples]
depends: [deploy.canary.workflow]
see_also: [platform.stack]
summary: example grafana alert configuration for canary deployment monitoring
detail:
  Dashboard is Production Canary Monitoring at grafana.internal/d/canary-monitor.
  Panel 1 shows Request Success Rate by Version using
  sum(rate(http_requests_total{status!~"5.."}[5m])) by (version)
  divided by sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m])) by (version) times 100.
  Alert fires if success_rate below 99% for any version over 2 minutes.
  Panel 2 shows P99 Latency by Version using histogram_quantile 0.99
  on sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])) by (le, version).
  Alert fires if p99 above 500ms for canary version over 3 minutes.
  Panel 3 shows Error Rate Delta computed as
  (canary_error_rate - baseline_error_rate) / baseline_error_rate * 100.
  Alert fires if delta above 10% sustained for 5 minutes.
  Notification channel is PagerDuty oncall-platform.

node rollout.orchestration
type: orchestration
stability: medium
priority: critical
confidence: high
summary: coordinate e-commerce platform deployment in dependency order with parallel frontend and backend tracks
parallel_groups:
  - group: 1-database-migrations
    nodes: [inventory.reserve]
    strategy: sequential
  - group: 2-backend-services
    nodes: [order.lifecycle, payment.process, shipping.estimation]
    strategy: parallel
    requires: [1-database-migrations]
    max_concurrency: 2
  - group: 3-verification
    nodes: [deploy.canary.workflow]
    strategy: sequential
    requires: [2-backend-services]
detail:
  The deployment orchestration ensures database migrations run before
  any service deployment. Backend services can deploy in parallel with
  bounded concurrency to avoid overwhelming the cluster. Frontend
  deployment depends on backend stability verification. The canary
  process acts as the final gate before full rollout.
memory:
  - action: upsert
    topic: deployment.tracking
    key: deployment.current.phase
    value: orchestration started
    scope: project
    ttl: permanent
  - action: upsert
    topic: deployment.tracking
    key: deployment.start.time
    value: 2024-09-15T14:30:00Z
    scope: session
